    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193308": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Teaching Fellowship in Electrical and Computer Engineering",
      "description": "Apply - Interfolio undefined page loaded Skip to Main Content Already have an account? Sign In Postdoctoral Teaching Fellowship in Electrical and Computer Engineering Carnegie Mellon University: College of Engineering: Electrical and Computer Engineering Location Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania, USA Open Date Sep 10, 2026 Description The Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering at Carnegie Mellon University invites applications for a Postdoctoral Teaching Fellowship designed to prepare recent Ph.D. graduates for faculty careers centered on teaching, student mentorship, and educational leadership. We welcome applicants interested in all areas of electrical engineering, computer engineering, and closely related fields. The fellowship combines substantive classroom experience with structured mentorship from ECE faculty alongside college and university faculty development programming. Fellows will develop their capabilities as engineering educators through teaching, course and laboratory development, and reflection on student learning. We welcome applicants who are enthusiastic about helping students connect foundational concepts with practical engineering problems and who aspire to teaching-track faculty positions or faculty careers at institutions with a strong teaching mission. Fellows will teach and co-teach undergraduate and/or graduate ECE courses aligned with their expertise and departmental needs, with opportunities to serve as instructor of record. Teaching assignments will be planned to provide increasing independence while preserving significant time for professional development. Each fellow will work with one or more designated faculty mentors to establish an individualized development plan. The fellowship will include regular mentoring meetings, classroom observation and feedback, and opportunities to develop course materials, laboratory experiences, assignments, and assessments. Fellows will also receive guidance in assembling a teaching portfolio, preparing faculty application materials, and developing teaching demonstrations. Teaching and preparing for a teaching faculty career are the fellowship’s primary activities. Fellows may pursue other scholarly activities consistent with their professional goals and fellowship activities. Applicants must hold a Ph.D. in electrical engineering, computer engineering, a computer science, or a closely related field by the appointment start date. Candidates should demonstrate strong disciplinary preparation, clear communication, and, most importantly, a thoughtful interest in how students learn and a commitment to supporting students with varied backgrounds. Evidence of teaching potential may come from teaching assistantships, tutoring, mentoring, outreach, curriculum development, or other educational activities. It is understood that such experience will be nascent compared to that of teaching faculty candidates. This is a full-time, academic year appointment beginning fall 2027. It is potentially renewable for up to two (2) years in total, but there is no built-in expectation of renewal. Summer support may potentially be available and can be discussed alongside a plan for summer activity. The benefits package and job framework are consistent with the University policies, practices, and offerings for the underlying special faculty appointment. About the Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering The Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering (ECE) at Carnegie Mellon University brings together education and research across the foundations and applications of electrical and computer engineering. Its curriculum connects electronic devices and circuits, digital systems, signal processing, computing, and software systems, combining mathematical and scientific foundations with laboratory work and engineering design. The department offers undergraduate, master’s, and doctoral programs and maintains extensive connections with multidisciplinary institutes, research centers, and collaborators worldwide. Students and faculty benefit from advanced experimental and computing infrastructure, including nanofabrication facilities, and rich collaborations and collaborators across campus and beyond. This breadth provides a rich setting for educators interested in helping students connect theory, experimentation, and the design of practical systems. About the College of Engineering, Carnegie Institute of Technology Carnegie Mellon’s College of Engineering (CIT) combines technical depth with a commitment to creativity, interdisciplinary collaboration, and the societal impact of engineering. The college encompasses biomedical, chemical, civil and environmental, electrical and computer, materials science, and mechanical engineering, as well as engineering and public policy. Its Information Networking Institute and Integrated Innovation Institute further connect engineering with emerging technologies, product development, and professional practice. Faculty and students collaborate across engineering and with colleagues in the sciences, arts, and business to address challenges in areas such as health, energy, manufacturing, and artificial intelligence. With educational and research activities extending to Silicon Valley and Africa, the college offers a global perspective on engineering practice. Its mission is to prepare technically accomplished and creative engineers who can work across disciplines and translate new knowledge into solutions that benefit society. About Carnegie Mellon University Carnegie Mellon University (CMU) is a globally elite private research university with internationally recognized strengths spanning engineering, the computer sciences, AI/ML, business, fine arts, wet sciences, and the humanities and social sciences. Its academic culture combines rigorous inquiry with an entrepreneurial spirit and a willingness to pursue ideas across traditional disciplinary boundaries. The university boasts a global prominence through national and international campuses and program presences anchored at its original home campus in Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania. Qualifications Applicants must hold a Ph.D. in electrical engineering, computer engineering, a computer science, or a closely related field by the appointment start date. Candidates should demonstrate strong disciplinary preparation, clear communication, and, most importantly, a thoughtful interest in how students learn and a commitment to supporting students with varied backgrounds. Evidence of teaching potential may come from teaching assistantships, tutoring, mentoring, outreach, curriculum development, or other educational activities. It is understood that such experience will be nascent compared to that of teaching faculty candidates. Application Instructions Cover letter describing the applicant's interest in a teaching-focused faculty career and identifying their specific educational and engineering interests Curriculum vitae (CV). Teaching statement describing the applicant's approach to student learning, relevant educational experiences, and goals for development during the fellowship. Applicants with limited teaching experience may discuss their specific interests and aspirations instead of, or to augment, their experiences. Information about three (3) references who can support the application, including their name, email address, professional role or position and institution, and relationship to the applicant. Optional: Examples of teaching materials or other evidence of educational engagement. Applications should be submitted via the provided Interfolio link. Review will begin immediately and continue until openings are filled or March 31, 2027. Application Process This institution is using Interfolio's Faculty Search to conduct\n            this search. Applicants to this position receive a free Dossier\n            account and can send all application materials, including\n            confidential letters of recommendation, free of charge. Apply Now Equal Employment Opportunity Statement Carnegie Mellon University is an equal opportunity employer. It does not discriminate in admission, employment, or administration of its programs or activities on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, disability, age, sexual orientation, gender identity, pregnancy or related condition, family status, marital status, parental status, religion, ancestry, veteran status, or genetic information. Furthermore, Carnegie Mellon University does not discriminate and is required not to discriminate in violation of federal, state, or local laws or executive orders. © 2026 Elsevier Inc. or its licensors and contributors. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies. Support | Careers | Accessibility Policy | Cookies Settings | Privacy Policy | Terms of Service We use cookies that are necessary to make our site work. We may also use additional cookies to analyze, improve, and personalize our content and your digital experience. You can manage your cookie preferences using the Cookie settings link. For more information, see our Cookie Policy Cookie settings Accept all cookies Opt-Out Request Honored Cookie Preference Center We use cookies which are necessary to make our site work. We may also use additional cookies to analyse, improve and personalise our content and your digital experience. For more information, see our Cookie Policy and the list of Google Ad-Tech Vendors . You may choose not to allow some types of cookies. However, blocking some types may impact your experience of our site and the services we are able to offer. See the different category headings below to find out more or change your settings. You may also be able to exercise your privacy choices as described in our Privacy Policy Allow all Manage Consent Preferences Strictly Necessary Cookies Always active These cookies are necessary for the website to function and cannot be switched off in our systems. They are usually only set in response to actions made by you which amount to a request for services, such as setting your privacy preferences, logging in or filling in forms. You can set your browser to block or alert you about these cookies, but some parts of the site will not then work. Cookie Details List‎ Functional Cookies Functional Cookies These cookies enable the website to provide enhanced functionality and personalisation. They may be set by us or by third party providers whose services we have added to our pages. If you do not allow these cookies then some or all of these services may not function properly. Cookie Details List‎ Performance Cookies Performance Cookies These cookies allow us to count visits and traffic sources so we can measure and improve the performance of our site. They help us to know which pages are the most and least popular and see how visitors move around the site. Cookie Details List‎ Targeting Cookies Targeting Cookies These cookies may be set through our site by our advertising partners. They may be used by those companies to build a profile of your interests and show you relevant adverts on other sites. If you do not allow these cookies, you will experience less targeted advertising. Cookie Details List‎ Back Button Cookie List Search Icon Filter Icon Clear checkbox label label Apply Cancel Consent Leg.Interest checkbox label label checkbox label label checkbox label label Confirm my choices"
